

Point 3: Should we convert Module only, or both Module + Severity to LLM-based?

My recommendation: Neither should become a pure LLM call. Convert both to a hybrid (deterministic-first, LLM-as-fallback) — but only for the specific weak paths, not uniformly.

Here's the reasoning, broken down by field:

Module — current weak point

Looking at _resolve_module() again:

Tickets & Runbooks: module_reported is an explicit field already populated by the source system (a dropdown value in ServiceNow, a wiki metadata field). This is near-ground-truth. Deterministic matching here is correct behavior — don't touch it.
Release Notes: uses raw.affected_modules[0] if raw.affected_modules else raw.product. If affected_modules is empty or product doesn't closely resemble a canonical module string (e.g., product = "InsuranceSuite" or a version label), the fuzzy substring match fails and it silently defaults to PolicyCenter at confidence 0.3. That's not "low confidence, still roughly right" — it can be actively wrong, and PolicyCenter becomes a dumping ground for anything ambiguous.
Chat/RCA: same module_reported free-text field, same fuzzy-match risk.

So the actual problem isn't "module tagging is weak everywhere" — it's specifically the fallback branch (confidence 0.3) that defaults to PolicyCenter. That's the one place an LLM reading the narrative text (title/body) would clearly outperform a string match.

Severity — current weak points

Tickets: priority (P1-P4) is an authoritative, structured field from the ticketing tool. Keep deterministic — an LLM guessing severity from narrative text would be a downgrade here, not an improvement.
Chat/RCA with a linked ticket: inherits severity from the ticket (confidence 0.95). Also solid — keep deterministic.
Chat/RCA with NO linked ticket: single keyword check ("outage" in text) → SEV2_HIGH or SEV3_MEDIUM. Brittle — misses "down," "crash," "unavailable," "degraded," "unreachable," etc.
Release notes: note_type → fixed severity mapping (confidence 0.75). Coarse but defensible as a starting heuristic (vendor categorization already does real work here).
Runbooks: keyword check ("outage" or "unresponsive") → SEV1 or SEV3 (confidence 0.55). Same brittleness as the RCA fallback, and it's the most arbitrary of the four source handlers.

Notice the pattern: every path that's already self-reporting low confidence (0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.55) is exactly the path that's weak, and every path with high confidence (0.95, 1.0) is backed by a genuinely authoritative field. That's a useful signal — it means the fix is targeted, not a wholesale redesign.

Why not just make everything LLM-based?

Cost/latency: you already pay one LLM call per record for category+resolution_type. Making module+severity also LLM calls either doubles the API cost per record, or (better) means folding module+severity into the same structured output schema (_TaggingResult already has 4 fields — extending to 6 is trivial and keeps it at one call per record). This matters either way.
Auditability/determinism: a ticket's priority field or a runbook's module_reported is ground truth that exists in the source system already. Routing that through a probabilistic model (even at temperature=0) is a regression in traceability — "why is this SEV1?" should be answerable by "because the ticketing tool said P1," not "because the LLM inferred it from the text."
The low-confidence paths are already flagged: the codebase's own confidence scores (0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.55) tell you precisely where to intervene. Replacing the high-confidence deterministic paths would mean throwing away a stronger signal in favor of a weaker, more expensive one.

Proposed approach (for your eventual implementation — not now)

Extend _TaggingResult (and the single _llm_classify() call) to also return module and severity picks + their own confidence/reasoning, so there's zero extra API cost.
Use the LLM's pick only when the deterministic resolver's confidence is below a threshold (e.g., module fallback at 0.3, severity fallback at ≤0.5/0.55) — otherwise keep the deterministic value and ignore the LLM's module/severity output.
This targets exactly the PolicyCenter-default problem and the keyword-only severity guesses, while leaving the ticket-priority and ticket-linked-RCA paths untouched.

Net answer to your question: convert both module and severity, but as a confidence-gated fallback, not a full replacement — module's weak spot is release notes/ambiguous free text, severity's weak spot is the "no linked ticket" / keyword-only branches in chat-RCA and runbooks.



Point 4: Selective Ingestion + Duplicate-Run Prevention

Agreed this is missing today. Two separate problems here that need separate mechanisms:

Problem A: Selective source ingestion

ingest_all() always loads and processes all 4 JSON files unconditionally. Need a way to say "only run chat_rca + release_notes this time."

Design consideration to flag now (important dependency): normalize_chat_or_rca() needs ticket_index to inherit severity from linked tickets (the 0.95-confidence path). If a selective run excludes tickets, that index would be empty for this run. Two options:

Build ticket_index from the KB's already-stored ticket_records table (query existing severities) rather than only from tickets freshly ingested in this run.
Or require tickets always be loaded for indexing purposes, even if not written out — but that defeats some of the "selective" intent if tickets.json is large.

I'd lean toward the first option — querying stored ticket severities from Postgres as a fallback index — since it keeps the selective run genuinely independent of what else happens to be in data/raw/.

Problem B: Don't reprocess (re-tag, re-embed) unchanged records on every run

This is the bigger one, and it's not fully solved by what already exists. Let's be precise about what's already handled vs. not:

Already handled:

insert_records_no_embedding()'s ON CONFLICT (record_id) DO UPDATE — won't create duplicate rows.
Step 4's WHERE embedding IS NULL — won't regenerate embeddings for rows that already have one.

NOT handled — this is the actual gap:

Every run still re-does the LLM tagging call (_llm_classify()) for every record, every time, even if the raw source record hasn't changed at all. That's the expensive, wasteful part you're flagging — not the embedding (already skip-safe) but the classification call.
There's no concept of "this record was already fully processed in a prior run and nothing about it has changed, so skip it entirely" — ingestion currently has no memory of prior runs.

Proposed mechanism: content-hash-based skip, checked before the LLM call

Add a lightweight tracking table (e.g. ingestion_state: record_id, source_type, content_hash, last_ingested_at, kb_version) — separate from the 4 content tables, so it's one place to check regardless of source.
For each raw record loaded, compute a hash of its raw fields (not the normalized/tagged output — the input).
Before calling _llm_classify(): look up record_id in ingestion_state. If the stored hash matches the freshly computed one, skip tagging entirely — pull forward the already-stored tags/SOP/embedding from the KB untouched, don't call the LLM, don't touch the embedding.
Only records that are new (record_id not seen before) or changed (hash differs) go through the full tag → structure → embed path.
Update ingestion_state for every record that did get processed.

This is where the real "don't execute again and again" savings live — skipping the Azure OpenAI classify call (and, by extension, never generating an embedding for it either, since step 4 already won't touch populated embeddings) for the ~100% of records that haven't changed between runs.

Two edge cases worth flagging for later, since they'd silently break the hash-skip logic if ignored:

Taxonomy changes: if you later add a new IssueCategory value, previously-tagged records won't get retagged under this, since their raw content hasn't changed — only the available tag set has. Needs a secondary version marker (e.g. taxonomy_version) stored alongside the hash, bumped whenever taxonomy.py changes, forcing a retag even when content is identical.
Prompt/model changes: same issue if _TAGGING_PROMPT or the Azure deployment changes — you'd want a way to force re-tagging without having to touch every raw record. A --force flag at the pipeline level (bypass the hash check entirely for a manual full refresh) covers this.

Summary of what I'd actually build (when you're ready to implement)

ingest_all(sources: list[SourceType] | None = None) — selective source loading, with ticket_index sourced from the DB when tickets aren't in the selected set.
An ingestion_state tracking table + content-hash check inserted right before _llm_classify() in each normalize_*() function — this is the actual "don't run again and again" mechanism.
A --force escape hatch to bypass the hash check for deliberate full re-tagging (prompt/taxonomy changes).
CLI flags on pipeline.py / step3_populate_data.py: --sources tickets,chat_rca and --force.

Let me know if you want me to proceed with implementing this, or if you'd like to adjust the approach first (e.g., threshold values for the confidence-gated LLM fallback, or whether you'd rather track hashes per-source-table instead of a central table).







